 "rulebook": "FIG Apparatus Norms (F\u00e9d\u00e9ration Internationale de Gymnastique), women's artistic gymnastics, balance beam: length 5.00 m, width of the top 0.10 m, depth of the beam body 0.16 m, top 1.25 m above the floor; FIG Code of Points, women's artistic gymnastics: a beam exercise lasts at most 1 min 30 s",
 "deviations": [
  "FIG Apparatus Norms, balance beam (padding): the beam is rigid with a friction coefficient of 1.0, without the norm's thin elastic padding and suede cover, because the simulator has rigid bodies only",
  "FIG Apparatus Norms, landing mats: there are no 0.20 m mats round the beam; the floor is 1.25 m below the top, because anything touching the floor ends the attempt anyway",
  "FIG Code of Points (a routine is scored, a fall costs 1.00): one pass along the beam is judged pass or fail, because the task is a single action; a hand or any part other than a foot on the beam counts as a fall here"
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Walk along the balance beam from the end you stand on to the other end and stand there, without anything touching the floor.",
  "rules": "The beam is a competition balance beam: 5.0 m long, 0.1 m wide on top and 0.16 m deep, its top 1.25 m above the floor; it runs along +x from x = 0 to x = 5.0 m, centred on y = 0, on two supports. You must get from the end you start on to the far end on your feet. The far end is the last 0.5 m of the beam. You succeed when both feet are on the beam with every point at which they touch it inside the far end, and you then stand there for 2.0 s without a break: only your feet touch the beam, your torso is within 20.0 degrees of vertical and your pelvis moves slower than 0.35 m/s. You fail the moment any part of you touches the floor or the beam's supports (a fall from the beam), the moment any part of you other than a foot touches the beam (a fall on the beam: no hands, knees or sitting), or after 90 s. Leaving the beam with both feet (a hop) is allowed as long as you come down on it on your feet.",
  "venue": "At t = 0 you stand on the beam's near end, facing along it (+x), in a tandem stance: the left foot in front of the right, both on the beam's centre line, the feet's centres 0.15 m and 0.39 m from the near end (the soles reach from 0.09 m to 0.52 m); your pelvis is 0.25 m from the near end and 0.75 m above the beam's top, so your soles are 1.25 m above the floor. The beam is narrower than your normal stance (your feet are normally 0.24 m apart; each sole is 0.07 m wide), so here the robot does NOT start in `stand` mode: it starts with all its joints holding the tandem pose on the default gains, as after a `joints` row, and the hold command at t = 0 is a `lowcmd` row that keeps those targets (left alone, the robot stays standing like that). A `stand` row would move the legs to the normal stance and a `walk` row would start the normal gait: both put the feet beside the beam. The beam and its two supports (0.9 m from each end) are fixed; the far end is marked yellow.",
  "difficulty": "The beam is 0.1 m wide and the robot's built-in stance and gait keep the feet 0.24 m apart, so neither can be used: every step is a single-leg balance on a sole 0.07 m wide, 1.25 m above the floor, for 5.0 m. No controller of this robot is known to do it."
 },
